Quick Sort The Quicksort algorithm is quite efficient, with an average time complexity of O(n log n). If implemented properly, it can also provide a worst-case time complexity of O(n log n). However, the worst-case scenario is not often encountered with randomized data, but it can occur if the partition process always splits the data into two data sets of very different sizes. This results in a highly inefficient algorithm, like a case where each recursive call splits the data only into two sections and one of them is always empty. To prevent this from happening the Quicksort implementation needs to do a good job at choosing pivot elements. An excellent pivot element can result in log(n) recursive calls each doing log(n) work, i.e., the time complexity can improve to O(n log n). If elements are being sorted in order, then a particular worst case scenario occurs and the time complexity can be O(n^2). However, this worst case is very exceptional and it occurs if we always take the first or the last element as a pivot.
